# Expectation record for 03_initializers/004_designated_array.c
#
# Designated array initializers: sparse and out-of-order designators, the C11
# current-object repositioning rule, implied array size, constant-expression
# indices, two-dimensional designators, array-of-struct designators, and
# overlapping designators whose later-initializer precedence is observable.
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 004_designated_array
area               = 03_initializers
description        = Designated array initializers -- sparse and out-of-order designators, designated-then-positional continuation exercising the C11 current-object rule, array size implied by the largest designator, constant-expression indices, two-dimensional row and element designators, array-of-struct designators, and OVERLAPPING designators in five forms whose later-initializer precedence is observable, including a discriminating pair that spells the same two initializers in opposite order -- for static and automatic storage duration, every element read back individually
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_audit_source_suppressions = -Woverride-init
ub_notes           <<END
This program contains no undefined and no unspecified behaviour. That is the precondition which
makes both differential oracles sound: undefined behaviour would license either compiler to do
anything at all, so a divergence over it would prove nothing about either one.

The one rule this program has to satisfy that an ordinary initializer program does not is C11
6.7.9p23: the evaluations of an initializer list's expressions are indeterminately sequenced with
respect to one another, so the order in which their SIDE EFFECTS occur is unspecified. Every
initializer expression here is an integer constant expression -- 1, 10 through 13, 20, 30, 50, 70,
101 through 103, 110 through 113, 120, 130, 170, and the small constants in the two-dimensional and
struct cases -- and a constant expression has no side effects at all, so that unspecified order is
unobservable. Which SLOT each initializer lands in is a separate rule, fixed by C11 6.7.9p19, which
initializes in initializer-list order; that is what several of these forms depend on for their
answer, and it is not what 6.7.9p23 leaves open.

THAT DISTINCTION IS WHAT MAKES THE OVERLAPPING FORMS LEGITIMATE HERE, so it is worth being precise
about. p19 requires the initialization to occur in initializer-list order and makes each initializer
provided for a particular subobject override any previously listed initializer for the same
subobject, so a subobject named twice has exactly one standard-mandated value: the one the source
listed last. p23 constrains something different -- WHEN the expressions are evaluated -- and it
matters only when an expression has a side effect. Every overridden and overriding initializer in
this program is a constant, so there is no side effect to sequence, and the two paragraphs cannot
interact. An override carrying a side effect would be a different matter and is excluded for that
reason rather than for any gate reason: p23 would leave the order of those side effects unspecified,
which requirement 1 forbids outright. Overriding is also not a double modification of an object:
p19 describes which initializer supplies a subobject's value, and an initializer list is not a
sequence of assignments performed on a live object.

Every object is fully initialized before any read. Elements not named by a designator are implicitly
initialized to zero under C11 6.7.9p19 and p21, so every gap between sparse designators holds a
definite zero rather than an indeterminate value. There is no read of uninitialized storage. That
applies to the two automatic-duration objects exactly as it does to the nine static ones, because an
object with any initializer at all is fully initialized.

EVERY DESIGNATOR INDEX IS IN RANGE. g_sparse and l_sparse are declared [8] and use
indices 0, 3 and 7. g_continue, l_continue and g_constexpr are declared [6] and use
indices 0, 1, 2 and 3, with the positional initializers following [1] landing at
[2] and [3]. g_implied is declared with an unspecified bound, and C11 6.7.9p22
makes its size the largest designated index plus one, so its length is exactly 5
and index 4 is its last element. The two-dimensional objects are declared [2][3]
and use row indices 0 and 1 and element indices 0, 1 and 2. g_st_memb and
g_st_brace are declared [3] and use indices 0 and 2. Among the overriding objects,
g_over_flat and l_over_flat are declared [4] and use indices 0, 1 and 2;
g_over_row_elem and g_over_elem_row are declared [2][3] and use row [0] and element
[0][1]; g_over_memb is declared [2] and uses indices 0 and 1 with the members .p and
.q. Every index an override names is therefore strictly inside its declared bounds,
and the elements no initializer names -- g_over_flat[3], row [1] of both
two-dimensional objects, g_over_memb[1].q and l_over_flat[3] -- are zero by p19 and
p21 rather than indeterminate.

The two constant-expression indices [2 + 1] and [1 * 2] are integer constant
expressions evaluated at translation time to 3 and 2, both within the declared
bound of 6. No signed overflow is possible in either.

No arithmetic is performed on any initialized value. The only run-time arithmetic
is on the loop counters i and j, each bounded strictly below its extent. Every
subscript is in bounds; no one-past-end pointer is formed and none is
dereferenced.

No shifts, no pointer casts, no aliasing -- each object is read only through its
own declared type. Nothing is modified twice between two sequence points. The
initialized aggregates are never modified after their initializers at all, so
every value printed is the value its initializer gave it; the only objects that
are modified are the loop counters i and j, each assigned in the initial clause
of its own for statement and incremented in that statement's controlling
expression. Every one of those assignments and increments is a full expression of
its own, so each is separated from every other access to the counter by a
sequence point, and each counter is bounded by a literal or by a sizeof-derived
extent equal to the declared one, so neither overflows and neither leaves an
array. Each printf call has at most one argument with a side effect, and none has
one. No padding byte is read: only named members are accessed, no struct is
printed as a whole, and no object representation is inspected.

g_implied_len is computed as (int)(sizeof g_implied / sizeof g_implied[0]). The two
sizeof operands cancel, so this is an ELEMENT COUNT rather than a byte size and is
5 on every target irrespective of pointer or long width. The read loop over g_implied uses that
same computed count rather than a literal 5, so an array the compiler sized wrongly shows up as a
wrong g_implied_len line rather than as a read past the end of the object.

Every conversion specifier is %d with an argument of type int after the default argument promotions,
so no variadic argument is read at the wrong width. main returns 0, inside the 0-125 range the suite
requires so that no exit status is truncated by the operating system. Nothing outside the source file
is consulted: every input is a literal here, no file, socket, environment variable, clock, random
source or locale is read, and iteration order is fixed, so every cell is reproducible from this
program and this record alone.

No header is included anywhere and printf is declared by hand as `int printf(const char *, ...);`.
That is the corpus-wide rule stated in tests/conformance/README.md, whose two sanctioned exceptions
are area 07's stdarg.h and area 12's bundled-header probe; area 03 takes neither.

This record declares no COMMAND-LINE warning-gate deviation, and area 03 sanctions none: no
ub_audit_flags key appears, so the program is compiled under the full default gate -Wall -Wextra
-pedantic -Wconversion -Wsign-conversion -Wshadow -Werror at -O0, -O1 and -O2 with all four reference
drivers, and it builds and runs clean under the sanitizer gate -fsanitize=undefined,address
-fno-sanitize-recover=all. Both gates are reference-compiler-only and are never applied to bcc, so
requirement 3's shared-flag discipline is untouched.

The translation unit does contain diagnostic-control directives, and they are REGISTERED rather than
merely mentioned: the ub_audit_source_suppressions key above names -Woverride-init, which is the one
diagnostic two matched push/pop pairs suppress, around the five overriding declarations and nothing
else. That key is machine-read. The audit scans this program's source on every run and fails the
warning gate if it finds a directive this record does not register, if this record registers a warning
the source does not suppress, if a suppression is not bracketed by a matched push and pop, if the
warning is not sanctioned for this exact area and program, or if the reason below does not name it --
so the exception is enforced on the same terms as a command-line deviation rather than resting on this
paragraph. -Werror remains in force, every other diagnostic remains fatal, and -Woverride-init itself
remains fatal everywhere outside those two bracketed regions, which was verified by measurement.

REASONING ON OVERLAPPING DESIGNATORS, AS THE AREA REQUIREMENTS DIRECT:

The area requirements permit overlapping designators "only where the later
initializer's precedence is well defined". Two distinct situations must be
separated here.

First, the situation this program DOES exercise. In g_continue the initializer
{ [1] = 11, 12, 13, [0] = 10 } contains no two initializers for the same element.
The designator [1] repositions the current object; the following positional
initializers 12 and 13 therefore initialize [2] and [3] under C11 6.7.9p17 and
p18; the trailing designator [0] then repositions again and initializes [0]
without disturbing anything already set. Elements [4] and [5] are implicitly zero.
The resulting order 10, 11, 12, 13, 0, 0 is fully determined by the standard, so
this is a well-defined use of interleaved designated and positional initializers
and it is included. The same reasoning applies to the two-dimensional objects
g_row_then_elem and g_elem_then_row, whose designators address disjoint elements
and which therefore produce identical results from opposite initializer orders.
Four further forms turn on the same rule without naming a slot twice: g_elem_only supplies three
element designators out of row order, g_st_brace lists its element braces [2] before [0], g_st_memb
interleaves member designators across two elements, and g_sparse and l_sparse designate descending
indices 7, 3, 0. Each is gate-clean precisely because no initializer in it revisits a slot an
earlier one already named.

Second, the situation this program ALSO EXERCISES, which is the genuine overlap: a designator that
supplies a SECOND initializer for a subobject an earlier initializer already named. C11 6.7.9p19
makes the later initializer win, so the answer is fully determined and the "only where the later
initializer's precedence is well defined" condition is met exactly. Five objects carry it, and each
was chosen because it fails differently if initializer order is mishandled.

g_over_flat = { [0] = 1, [1] = 2, [1] = 20, [2] = 3 } is the flat form: index [1] is named twice, so
it must read 20 and not 2. A front end that folded designators into a map keyed by index would keep
whichever value it happened to insert last, which need not be the one the source listed last.

g_over_row_elem = { [0] = { 1, 2, 3 }, [0][1] = 20 } names a whole row and then one element inside
it, so the element designator must override just that element and leave [0][0] and [0][2] at 1 and 3.
g_over_elem_row = { [0][1] = 20, [0] = { 1, 2, 3 } } names the same element and then the same whole
row, so the row brace must override the earlier element and [0][1] must read 2.

THOSE TWO ARE THE DISCRIMINATING PAIR, and they are the reason this coverage is worth having rather
than merely present. They contain the IDENTICAL two initializers in OPPOSITE order. Under the
standard's rule -- the last initializer to name a subobject wins -- element [0][1] reads 20 in the
first and 2 in the second. Under the weaker rule an implementation might accidentally implement --
any explicitly named subobject survives -- it would read 20 in BOTH. A single pair of golden lines,
g_over_row_elem[0][1]=20 and g_over_elem_row[0][1]=2, therefore separates the correct rule from the
plausible wrong one, and neither line can be produced by an implementation holding the other rule.

g_over_memb = { [0] = { 1, 2 }, [0].q = 20, [1].p = 3 } overrides one MEMBER of an array-of-struct
element whose whole value an earlier brace supplied, so the override has to descend into the element
rather than replace it: .p must stay 1 while .q becomes 20. l_over_flat repeats the flat form at
AUTOMATIC duration, where the winning value is resolved by emitted code on entry to the block instead
of being settled into a data-section image -- the same language rule through a different code path,
which is where a backend rather than a front end could get it wrong.

HOW THE AUTHORING GATE IS SATISFIED, since this construct and the gate genuinely conflict. -Wextra
enables -Woverride-init and the gate's -Werror makes it fatal, and there is no gate-clean spelling of
a genuine override: every spelling measured against the reference compiler is rejected with
"initialized field overwritten [-Werror=override-init]" -- a duplicate element designator; an
override carrying the SAME value; a zero-then-value override; a whole-row brace then one element of
that row; that pair reversed; positional continuation running back over an earlier designator; a
struct-element brace then one member of it; a duplicate struct member designator; two union member
designators; a bitfield member; a nested whole-member brace then one member of it; the reverse of
that nesting; and an override inside a compound literal. The forms that DO pass the gate untouched
were checked too and are not overrides at all: { .v = { 0 }, .v[1] = 5 } overrides only an IMPLICIT
zero, so it demonstrates nothing about precedence.

So the one initializer diagnostic standing between this file and the construct the area requires is
suppressed FROM INSIDE, with a diagnostic-control directive both reference compilers implement,
around exactly the declarations that need it and no further, restoring the previous state on the next
line. THIS RECORD CLAIMS NO GATE DEVIATION AND THERE IS NONE: no ub_audit_flags key appears, the full
default gate -Wall -Wextra -pedantic -Wconversion -Wsign-conversion -Wshadow -Werror is applied
unmodified to this program, and -Werror stays in force throughout. The suppression's scope was
verified rather than assumed: an override written OUTSIDE the push/pop region is still rejected with
-Werror=override-init, and stripping the two directives makes the reference compiler report five
override diagnostics, so the suppression is both load-bearing and confined. This is the ONLY program
in the corpus that carries a diagnostic-control directive, and the concentration is the point: a
suppression scoped to a few declarations is strictly better than a deviation scoped to a translation
unit, because it is visible where it applies and it leaves the gate at full strength for this program
as for every other -- and confining the mechanism to the one program whose REQUIRED construct has no
gate-clean spelling means a divergence anywhere else in the corpus can never be an artefact of a
suppressed diagnostic. The admissibility test is the scope and the subject, not the mechanism: a
directive used to silence a diagnostic about the very behaviour a program exists to test would make
this record's gate claim false, and no program here does that. C11 6.10.6 governs the directives;
they change which messages the translator emits and nothing about the program's semantics, and each
is matched by a pop, so neither can affect a printed value or leak past the declarations it
brackets.

ONE SPELLING REMAINS EXCLUDED, and its reason is not the gate. An override whose initializer carries
a SIDE EFFECT is excluded because C11 6.7.9p23 leaves the sequencing of an initializer list's
evaluations unspecified, so the order of those side effects would be observable, which requirement 1
forbids outright. Every override above is a constant expression, so this program is unaffected by
that exclusion; -Woverride-init-side-effects, which is enabled by DEFAULT and is not a gate member at
all, correspondingly has nothing to report here.

A range designator such as { [0 ... 3] = 7 } is a different feature rather than another spelling of
this one: it is a GCC extension rather than C11, it is rejected by -pedantic which the gate also
enables -- measured, as "ISO C forbids specifying range of elements to initialize
[-Werror=pedantic]" -- and GCC extensions are the subject of area 08 rather than area 03.

The FEATURE under test -- designated array initializers -- is therefore covered without a gap:
sparse and out-of-order designators, designated-then-positional continuation, array size implied by
the largest designator, constant-expression indices, two-dimensional row-then-element and
element-then-row and element-only designators, array-of-struct designators in both member and brace
form, and overlapping designators in the flat, nested-both-directions, struct-member and
automatic-duration forms. Nothing is dropped for difficulty, and nothing is replaced by prose
describing why it is absent. The discriminating pair is what separates "the last initializer to name a
subobject wins" from the weaker "any explicitly named subobject survives", so neither rule can satisfy
this program by accident.

No expected-divergence marker is carried and none would be right. A marker classifies a divergence
against a documented limitation of the compiler under test; nothing above cites one, and the
diagnostic that had to be worked around belongs to the reference compiler's authoring gate rather
than to bcc. If bcc rejects or mis-orders any override here, that is an undocumented divergence and
must be delivered as a finding with its minimized reproducer, not classified away.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
This record narrows exactly one thing, and it narrows it from inside the program rather than on the
command line: all four targets, all three optimization levels and all three oracles are enabled, no
expected-divergence marker is carried, no gate deviation is claimed under the ub_audit_flags key, and
no implementation-defined value reaches this program's output -- but two matched push/pop pairs in the
source suppress -Woverride-init, and that is the narrowing this paragraph exists to justify. Every
initializer form the area requires is present in the source, overlapping designators included. The
single spelling still absent is an override whose initializer carries a SIDE EFFECT, and because that
exclusion is an undefined-and-unspecified-behaviour matter rather than an implementation-defined one,
it is stated where the area requirements direct: in ub_notes above, under REASONING ON OVERLAPPING
DESIGNATORS.

THE REASON FOR SUPPRESSING -Woverride-init, WHICH THE ub_audit_source_suppressions KEY REGISTERS.
-Woverride-init is enabled by -Wextra and made fatal by -Werror, and it fires on an initializer that
overrides an earlier one for the same subobject. That is not a defect here: it is the CONSTRUCT UNDER
TEST. C11 6.7.9p19 initializes in initializer-list order and gives the later initializer precedence,
so the five overriding objects -- g_over_flat, g_over_row_elem, g_over_elem_row, g_over_memb and the
automatic l_over_flat -- have exactly one correct answer each, and g_over_row_elem against
g_over_elem_row is the discriminating pair whose whole value is that the same two initializers in
opposite order must give different results. The diagnostic reports well-defined behaviour that the
area requirements oblige this program to exercise.

Neither alternative is acceptable, which is why the suppression is the narrowest available response.
Removing the objects would drop mandated initializer coverage because a diagnostic found a defined
construct surprising, which the corpus forbids. Dropping -Wextra from the gate through ub_audit_flags
would be a far wider relaxation -- it would discard every other diagnostic -Wextra carries for the
whole file -- and area 03 sanctions no gate deviation in any case. The directives instead bracket the
five declarations and nothing else with a matched push and pop, so -Woverride-init is fatal again on
the next line, and every other diagnostic is fatal throughout. The exception is bounded in the source,
registered in this record, sanctioned for this exact program in the harness's own table, and checked
in both directions on every run.

All printed values are int and are printed with %d only. The program contains no
long, size_t, ptrdiff_t, intptr_t, float, double or long double. The single sizeof
use is the cancelling element-count division for g_implied_len, which yields 5 on
every target, so the measured width difference between i686 (4-byte long and
pointer) and the other three targets (8-byte) cannot affect the output. That difference is
documented as well as measured: i686 is ELF32 with a four-byte long and pointer while x86_64,
aarch64 and riscv64 are ELF64 with eight bytes for both (docs/technical-specifications.md target
table, lines 457-462). No sizeof result is printed as a byte count and no _Alignof result is printed
at all.

The only character data is the format-string literals, whose bytes printf copies through
unchanged. No plain-char value is read as a number, converted, compared or formatted numerically,
so the measured plain-char signedness difference (signed on x86_64 and i686, unsigned on aarch64
and riscv64) cannot arise, and no character's numeric CODE is computed, so C11 5.2.1 leaves
nothing here to the execution character set either.

No address or pointer value is printed, and no struct is printed as a whole, so
padding bytes -- whose contents are unspecified -- are never observed. These all-int aggregates
carry no internal padding on any of the four targets in any case, and because no address is printed
no layout choice the ABI is free to make is observable: only the values the initializers supply are.

g_row_then_elem and g_elem_then_row are constructed to produce IDENTICAL output
from OPPOSITE initializer orders. Any asymmetry between those two line groups is
an unambiguous defect signal rather than an implementation-defined difference.

g_over_row_elem and g_over_elem_row are the mirror image of that construction and carry the opposite
obligation: the same two initializers in opposite order must produce DIFFERENT output, 20 and 2 at
element [0][1], because C11 6.7.9p19 gives the later initializer precedence. Equality between those
two line groups is therefore the defect signal, and it is not an implementation-defined difference in
either direction -- p19 fixes both answers, so neither target nor optimization level nor ABI has any
freedom here.

The discriminating variant here is the pairing of storage durations, which is also why all three
optimization levels matter: a static designated initializer is resolved before program startup and
emitted into the data image, whereas its automatic twin's placement is a run-time act the backend
performs on entry to the block, and at a higher level either path may be folded into immediate
operands. Every element of every object is read back individually, so a value landing in the wrong
slot, a disturbed neighbour or a lost zero fill is each its own line, and nothing is summarized.

Output is byte-identical on all four targets at all three optimization levels,
verified by measurement. The program was compiled with the reference driver for each target at -O0,
-O1 and -O2 -- twelve configurations -- and every one produced these 94 lines byte-identically with
exit status 0, which is why oracle (b) compares this program's targets against each other with no
narrowing and why oracle (c) can hold these bytes as the golden record. The twelve configurations
were re-measured after the overriding objects were added, so the 24 lines they contribute rest on the
same evidence as the other 70.
END
